WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.700
Portfolio, fair prices, and your first 90 days.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:08.080
This is the video for module four of Hair Care and Simple Styling.

3
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:16.260
New clients cannot feel how gentle your hands are, but they can see clean parts and healthy edges in good photos.

4
00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.710
Use daylight from a window, or outdoors in the shade.

5
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.160
Use a plain background. No unmade beds, kitchen pots or other people in the frame.

6
00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.150
Clean the lens with a soft cloth, so close-ups are not blurry.

7
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:41.340
Take the same angles every time: front, left, right, back, and a close-up of the parts and hairline. The back is where uneven parts show.

8
00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:50.370
Take a before set too. A photo a week later is powerful, because it shows your style stays neat and the hairline is calm.

9
00:00:51.620 --> 00:01:01.370
Always ask permission, write the answer on her card, and hide faces when asked. For children, ask the parent and never show the child's face publicly.

10
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:11.450
Pricing too low is one of the most common reasons small salons struggle. A low price fills your diary but leaves you tired and broke.

11
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.090
First, count materials: gel, cream, bands, and extension hair if you supply it.

12
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:27.600
Second, your overhead share. Add your monthly costs like disinfectant, washing, data and transport, and divide by the clients you expect.

13
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:34.160
Third, your time. Hours the style takes, multiplied by the hourly pay you want.

14
00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:44.160
Add them together. That is your minimum price. Then compare with good stylists near you, at least five of them, and round to a clear figure.

15
00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.740
Below that minimum, you are paying to work.

16
00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:59.100
Here is a worked example. Bisi in Lagos prices medium knotless braids, mid-back, with the client bringing her own hair. All figures are approximate.

17
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:04.680
Materials like gel, mousse and bands come to about 800 naira.

18
00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:13.670
Her overheads are about 15,000 naira a month across about 25 clients. That is 600 naira per client.

19
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:21.490
The style takes 5 hours, and she wants at least 3,000 naira an hour. That is 15,000 naira.

20
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:36.870
So her minimum price is 16,400 naira. Good braiders near her charge about 18,000 to 25,000, so she sets 20,000. That leaves about 3,600 for growth.

21
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:49.550
WhatsApp Business is a free app with a profile and a catalogue of services. Say what makes you different in one sentence, like: neat, gentle braids with clean tools for every client.

22
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.410
Show proof, and post one photo a few times a week rather than ten at once.

23
00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.860
Make booking easy with the price, any deposit, your account details and location.

24
00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:12.290
And be clear about your rules. If a client says the price is too high, do not argue. Offer a smaller option, like bigger braids or shorter length.

25
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:20.560
For long styles, a small deposit protects your time. Confirm it, and every transfer, in your own banking app.

26
00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:28.270
Write honest captions. Never post styles you did not do. Clients will notice when your work looks different.

27
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:41.960
And as you get busy, keep the rules: tools cleaned after every client, no styling over infection, no painful braids, and no chemical service without a scalp check, strand test and consent.

28
00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:51.530
Split your next 90 days into three blocks. Days 1 to 30: build skill and visibility, with your WhatsApp catalogue set up.

29
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:59.280
Days 31 to 60: steady paying clients, collect day-7 photos, and review your prices.

30
00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:06.040
Days 61 to 90: grow and stabilise. Make targets fit your real hours.

31
00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:16.370
For example, Hauwa in Abuja has about 56 hours a month. She plans 8 clients, then 12, then 15, leaving time for practice and rest.

32
00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:34.200
To remember. Same angles, daylight and a plain background. Consent before every photo. Price equals materials, plus overhead share, plus your time. Offer a smaller option instead of cutting your price. And set targets that fit your real hours.

33
00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:45.880
Now plan your portfolio, build your price list, and assemble your evidence pack. For the certificate, you need the capstone and at least 70 percent in the final quiz.

34
00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.170
Continue with the lessons below.
